{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790336694,"nonce":"0CVk8zAve67WQv3Rx3CGxO080Ns=","title":"Python: __name__ of a module an embedder loads is \"__main__\"","message":"parse_program_with names the main file's module __main__ (lib.rs, the Module built with name ENTRY), which is right for zypy running a script. An embedder that loads a Python file as one of its modules and runs its body (caribou runs a module's __main__ once on load) gets __name__ == \"__main__\" there too, so an 'if __name__ == \"__main__\":' block runs whenever another language imports the module. The embedder knows the module's dotted name; a way to pass it (a parse entry taking the module name, or a field of the resolver's options) would let __name__ be that name, as Python's import gives it.","files":null}]}